1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,580
Inside every electric car is a
box weighing around

2
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:04,390
half a tonne.

3
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:06,930
It's the most expensive part of
the car,

4
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:10,620
and it decides how far you can
drive, how fast you can

5
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:11,400
charge,

6
00:00:11,410 --> 00:00:12,950
and how long the car will last.

7
00:00:13,833 --> 00:00:17,283
That box is full of lithium-ion
cells, the

8
00:00:17,293 --> 00:00:19,953
same basic technology that's in
your phone.

9
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:23,880
But there's a new kind of
battery that could make electric
cars

10
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:24,690
lighter,

11
00:00:24,830 --> 00:00:25,630
safer,

12
00:00:25,990 --> 00:00:28,450
faster to charge, and able to go
much further.

13
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:30,730
It's called the solid-state
battery.

14
00:00:31,583 --> 00:00:34,793
Car makers are spending billions
to build it

15
00:00:34,803 --> 00:00:35,603
first.

16
00:00:35,783 --> 00:00:39,133
To understand why, we need to go
back to the very first

17
00:00:39,143 --> 00:00:39,943
battery.

18
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:44,040
In eighteen hundred, the Italian
scientist

19
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:47,400
Alessandro Volta stacked discs
of zinc and

20
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:48,210
copper,

21
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:50,890
separated by cloth soaked in
salty water.

22
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:54,260
It was the first battery, and it
produced a steady electric

23
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:55,070
current.

24
00:00:55,583 --> 00:00:57,903
Every battery since has worked
the same way.

25
00:00:58,503 --> 00:00:59,723
There are two electrodes:

26
00:01:00,363 --> 00:01:03,753
a negative one, called the
anode, and a

27
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:06,563
positive one, called the
cathode.

28
00:01:07,103 --> 00:01:08,823
Between them is an electrolyte.

29
00:01:09,417 --> 00:01:12,077
When you charge the battery,
tiny charged atoms,

30
00:01:12,497 --> 00:01:15,507
called ions, are pushed through
the electrolyte into the

31
00:01:15,517 --> 00:01:16,317
anode,

32
00:01:16,377 --> 00:01:19,204
where they're stored, like water
pumped uphill.

33
00:01:19,667 --> 00:01:23,057
When you use it, the ions flow
back, and the electrons they

34
00:01:23,067 --> 00:01:25,507
leave behind travel the long way
round,

35
00:01:25,767 --> 00:01:29,047
through the wire, powering your
phone, or your car's motor.

36
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:33,220
For over a century, the best
rechargeable battery was

37
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:36,680
the lead-acid kind, invented in
eighteen fifty nine

38
00:01:36,690 --> 00:01:40,010
and still used to start most
petrol cars today.

39
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:42,570
It worked, but it was enormously
heavy.

40
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,370
Early electric cars ran on these
batteries.

41
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:49,820
In nineteen hundred, about a
third of cars in the United
States

42
00:01:49,830 --> 00:01:50,630
were electric.

43
00:01:51,050 --> 00:01:53,450
But they couldn't go far, and
petrol won.

44
00:01:55,067 --> 00:01:56,787
The answer turned out to be
lithium.

45
00:01:57,407 --> 00:01:59,227
It's the lightest metal in the
universe,

46
00:01:59,747 --> 00:02:02,777
so light it floats on water, and
it gives up its

47
00:02:02,787 --> 00:02:06,147
electrons more eagerly than
almost any other element.

48
00:02:06,917 --> 00:02:09,777
In the nineteen seventies,
during an oil crisis,

49
00:02:10,237 --> 00:02:12,617
a British chemist named Stanley
Whittingham,

50
00:02:12,937 --> 00:02:16,127
working for the oil company
Exxon, built the first

51
00:02:16,137 --> 00:02:17,657
rechargeable lithium battery.

52
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:19,540
It had a big problem.

53
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:23,500
Its anode was pure lithium
metal, and every time it
charged,

54
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:27,460
the lithium grew tiny,
needle-like spikes called
dendrites.

55
00:02:28,333 --> 00:02:30,373
When a dendrite reached the
other side,

56
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:33,273
the battery short-circuited, and
could catch fire.

57
00:02:33,893 --> 00:02:37,363
Some early lithium-metal
batteries were recalled after
doing

58
00:02:37,373 --> 00:02:38,393
exactly that.

59
00:02:39,083 --> 00:02:41,063
In nineteen eighty, at Oxford,

60
00:02:41,723 --> 00:02:44,853
the American physicist John
Goodenough found a far

61
00:02:44,863 --> 00:02:47,703
better cathode: lithium cobalt
oxide.

62
00:02:48,663 --> 00:02:50,453
It nearly doubled the battery's
voltage,

63
00:02:50,463 --> 00:02:52,163
to about four volts.

64
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:55,663
Then in nineteen eighty five, in
Japan,

65
00:02:56,303 --> 00:02:59,473
the chemist Akira Yoshino
replaced the dangerous lithium

66
00:02:59,483 --> 00:03:01,123
metal anode with carbon.

67
00:03:01,823 --> 00:03:05,233
The lithium ions simply slipped
in between layers of carbon

68
00:03:05,243 --> 00:03:06,043
atoms,

69
00:03:06,303 --> 00:03:07,743
with no metal spikes to grow.

70
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:10,637
That was the modern lithium-ion
battery.

71
00:03:11,197 --> 00:03:14,167
In nineteen ninety one, Sony put
it on sale,

72
00:03:14,177 --> 00:03:15,977
inside a video camera.

73
00:03:16,750 --> 00:03:17,730
It changed everything.

74
00:03:18,390 --> 00:03:20,110
Mobile phones, laptops,

75
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:21,950
and eventually, cars.

76
00:03:22,917 --> 00:03:26,187
In twenty nineteen, Whittingham,
Goodenough and

77
00:03:26,197 --> 00:03:29,157
Yoshino shared the Nobel Prize
in Chemistry.

78
00:03:29,797 --> 00:03:33,267
Goodenough was ninety seven, the
oldest person ever to win

79
00:03:33,277 --> 00:03:34,297
a Nobel Prize.

80
00:03:34,877 --> 00:03:37,187
He kept working into his late
nineties,

81
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:39,185
and died at the age of one
hundred.

82
00:03:40,567 --> 00:03:43,937
In two thousand and eight, a
small company called

83
00:03:43,947 --> 00:03:47,431
Tesla built a sports car powered
by six thousand

84
00:03:47,441 --> 00:03:50,677
eight hundred and thirty one
laptop batteries,

85
00:03:50,687 --> 00:03:51,847
wired together.

86
00:03:52,667 --> 00:03:56,157
Since twenty ten, the price of
lithium-ion batteries has

87
00:03:56,167 --> 00:03:58,337
fallen by about ninety percent.

88
00:03:58,347 --> 00:04:01,557
In twenty twenty four, more than
seventeen million

89
00:04:01,567 --> 00:04:04,047
electric cars were sold around
the world,

90
00:04:04,407 --> 00:04:06,127
more than one in five new cars.

91
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,320
But today's batteries are
reaching their limits.

92
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:13,050
A big share of a battery pack's
weight is material

93
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:16,400
that doesn't store energy at
all: the casing,

94
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,120
the wiring, the cooling,

95
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:20,220
and the liquid electrolyte.

96
00:04:20,750 --> 00:04:22,550
That liquid is also flammable.

97
00:04:22,950 --> 00:04:26,220
If a cell is damaged, or
overheats, it can set

98
00:04:26,230 --> 00:04:28,990
off a chain reaction called
thermal runaway,

99
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:31,770
and that fire is very hard to
put out.

100
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:34,240
And charging is still slow.

101
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:36,220
Push the ions in too quickly,

102
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:39,320
and lithium starts plating onto
the anode as metal,

103
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:42,530
the same spiky dendrites that
haunted the first lithium

104
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:43,340
batteries.

105
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,090
So drivers worry about range,
and about waiting at

106
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:47,900
chargers.

107
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:50,620
Engineers wanted a battery that
was smaller,

108
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,940
safer, and could fill up in
minutes.

109
00:04:54,733 --> 00:04:57,423
The idea behind the solid-state
battery is simple.

110
00:04:57,433 --> 00:05:00,743
Replace the liquid electrolyte
with a thin layer of

111
00:05:00,753 --> 00:05:01,693
solid material,

112
00:05:02,313 --> 00:05:05,543
a special ceramic, glass, or
crystal that lets

113
00:05:05,553 --> 00:05:08,253
lithium ions pass straight
through it.

114
00:05:08,917 --> 00:05:12,257
A solid can't leak, and most of
these materials don't burn.

115
00:05:12,837 --> 00:05:16,207
So the battery needs far less
heavy protection around

116
00:05:16,217 --> 00:05:17,017
it.

117
00:05:17,083 --> 00:05:20,323
But the real prize is what a
solid electrolyte might allow
next.

118
00:05:21,123 --> 00:05:23,143
If it's strong enough to block
dendrites,

119
00:05:23,663 --> 00:05:26,733
engineers can finally go back to
Whittingham's dream:

120
00:05:26,743 --> 00:05:29,223
an anode made of pure lithium
metal.

121
00:05:29,917 --> 00:05:33,367
Lithium metal can hold around
ten times more charge than

122
00:05:33,377 --> 00:05:34,757
the same weight of graphite.

123
00:05:35,157 --> 00:05:38,247
Some designs go even further,
and start with no

124
00:05:38,257 --> 00:05:39,057
anode at all.

125
00:05:39,477 --> 00:05:42,417
The lithium builds it up the
first time the battery charges.

126
00:05:43,250 --> 00:05:46,680
The result could be a battery
that stores perhaps fifty
percent

127
00:05:46,690 --> 00:05:48,710
more energy in the same space.

128
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:51,660
That could mean a car that goes
much further,

129
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:54,570
or the same range from a
smaller, lighter,

130
00:05:55,570 --> 00:05:56,370
cheaper pack.

131
00:05:56,667 --> 00:05:59,547
And because the ions don't have
to dodge through a liquid,

132
00:06:00,027 --> 00:06:03,097
some solid-state cells could
charge from ten to eighty

133
00:06:03,107 --> 00:06:04,987
percent in around ten minutes.

134
00:06:06,733 --> 00:06:09,513
So why isn't every car already
using one?

135
00:06:10,013 --> 00:06:13,183
Because building a solid-state
battery turned out to be

136
00:06:13,193 --> 00:06:14,413
extraordinarily hard.

137
00:06:15,167 --> 00:06:17,847
A liquid touches every surface
it's poured onto.

138
00:06:18,487 --> 00:06:21,607
Two solids pressed together only
touch at tiny points,

139
00:06:22,007 --> 00:06:22,837
like two bricks.

140
00:06:22,847 --> 00:06:25,507
Ions struggle to cross those
gaps.

141
00:06:26,417 --> 00:06:29,667
Every time a battery charges,
its electrodes swell

142
00:06:29,677 --> 00:06:30,657
and shrink.

143
00:06:31,297 --> 00:06:33,357
A liquid flows to fill the
space.

144
00:06:33,997 --> 00:06:34,817
A solid cracks.

145
00:06:35,917 --> 00:06:37,887
And to the surprise of many
scientists,

146
00:06:37,897 --> 00:06:41,207
dendrites can still grow,
worming through

147
00:06:41,217 --> 00:06:44,177
tiny cracks and the boundaries
between crystals,

148
00:06:44,657 --> 00:06:46,017
even in hard ceramic.

149
00:06:46,833 --> 00:06:49,773
Some of the best solid
electrolytes are made with
sulfur.

150
00:06:50,533 --> 00:06:53,133
They let ions move almost as
fast as a liquid,

151
00:06:53,573 --> 00:06:56,903
but if they meet moisture in the
air, they can give off toxic

152
00:06:56,913 --> 00:06:58,353
hydrogen sulfide gas.

153
00:06:59,233 --> 00:07:01,093
So factories have to be bone
dry.

154
00:07:01,917 --> 00:07:04,177
Making a cell in a lab is one
thing.

155
00:07:04,557 --> 00:07:07,627
Making millions of them,
cheaply, with no defects,

156
00:07:07,637 --> 00:07:08,757
is another.

157
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,180
Even so, the race is on.

158
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:16,030
Toyota holds more solid-state
battery patents than

159
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:19,410
any other company, and says it
plans to put them

160
00:07:19,420 --> 00:07:22,527
in cars by around twenty twenty
seven or twenty

161
00:07:22,537 --> 00:07:23,370
twenty eight.

162
00:07:23,833 --> 00:07:27,083
In California, QuantumScape,
backed by

163
00:07:27,093 --> 00:07:27,893
Volkswagen,

164
00:07:28,353 --> 00:07:31,583
has been building a ceramic
separator thinner than a sheet
of

165
00:07:31,593 --> 00:07:32,003
paper,

166
00:07:32,013 --> 00:07:34,833
with cells that start with no
anode at all.

167
00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:38,670
In twenty twenty five,
Mercedes-Benz drove a test

168
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:41,890
car with solid-state cells from
Stuttgart to

169
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:42,350
Malmö,

170
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:45,150
in Sweden, more than twelve
hundred kilometres,

171
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:46,900
on a single charge.

172
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:51,163
In China, the car maker NIO
already sells cars

173
00:07:51,173 --> 00:07:54,283
with semi-solid batteries, a
halfway step,

174
00:07:54,293 --> 00:07:56,193
with some liquid still inside.

175
00:07:56,973 --> 00:08:00,383
In twenty twenty three, NIO's
founder drove one of

176
00:08:00,393 --> 00:08:03,683
his cars more than a thousand
kilometres on a single charge,

177
00:08:03,693 --> 00:08:04,993
live on the internet.

178
00:08:05,917 --> 00:08:07,357
And it isn't only about range.

179
00:08:08,177 --> 00:08:11,407
Every lithium battery needs
lithium, much of it mined in

180
00:08:11,417 --> 00:08:12,217
Australia,

181
00:08:12,537 --> 00:08:15,967
or pumped as salty water from
beneath the deserts of Chile and

182
00:08:15,977 --> 00:08:16,777
Argentina,

183
00:08:17,157 --> 00:08:20,557
and left to evaporate in giant
ponds for many months.

184
00:08:21,417 --> 00:08:24,437
A lithium-metal anode uses more
lithium per cell,

185
00:08:25,237 --> 00:08:27,587
so other researchers are racing
the other way,

186
00:08:27,597 --> 00:08:30,847
with sodium-ion batteries, made
from the same

187
00:08:30,857 --> 00:08:32,517
element as table salt.

188
00:08:33,137 --> 00:08:34,617
They store less, but they're
cheap.

189
00:08:35,250 --> 00:08:37,870
The likely future isn't one
perfect battery,

190
00:08:38,470 --> 00:08:41,480
but several: cheap ones for city
cars, and

191
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:44,820
solid-state ones for cars,
trucks, and maybe

192
00:08:44,830 --> 00:08:47,450
even planes, that need to go
much further.

193
00:08:49,233 --> 00:08:52,643
Two hundred years ago, Volta's
pile could barely make

194
00:08:52,653 --> 00:08:53,083
a spark.

195
00:08:53,093 --> 00:08:56,533
Today, batteries can carry a
family across a continent.

196
00:08:57,250 --> 00:08:59,550
The solid-state battery isn't
finished yet.

197
00:09:00,070 --> 00:09:03,480
But if engineers crack it, the
most important part of the

198
00:09:03,490 --> 00:09:06,150
electric car could be a thin
layer of solid,

199
00:09:06,610 --> 00:09:09,860
thinner than a sheet of paper,
that finally keeps lithium

200
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:10,950
in its place.
